# Code owners for openclaw-vercel-insights.
#
# GitHub matches these patterns bottom to top and applies the LAST match only,
# so the catch-all comes first and the deliberate exceptions come after it.
# A path listed here requires review from its owner before a pull request can
# merge into main, which is enforced by the "main-protection" ruleset.

# Everything, unless a rule further down claims it.
*                               @anatoli-iliev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The read-only guarantee
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# http.py is the whole security surface: the operation allowlist that decides
# which five endpoints exist and which verb each uses, the credential scrubbing,
# the header redaction and the refusal to follow redirects. A change here can
# quietly turn a read-only tool into something else, so it is called out
# separately rather than left to the catch-all.
/vercel_insights/http.py        @anatoli-iliev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the code is allowed to assume about Vercel's APIs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# api-notes.md separates what is verified against the live API from what is
# still inferred. Editing it changes the basis for request and response code
# elsewhere, and getting that wrong is how the scope object shipped wrong once
# already. cli-contract.md is the interface the tests are written against.
/docs/api-notes.md              @anatoli-iliev
/docs/cli-contract.md           @anatoli-iliev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Instructions users paste into a shell
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The setup sections of these two files are copy-paste blocks, including a
# chmod and a pip install. A change here runs on a reader's machine as surely
# as a change to the code does, and it is read by people who are trusting the
# instructions precisely because they cannot audit them.
/README.md                      @anatoli-iliev
/docs/openclaw-setup.md         @anatoli-iliev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release and supply chain
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI runs with no secrets and must stay that way. pyproject declares the single
# runtime dependency. SKILL.md is the published manifest: its frontmatter must
# keep declaring exactly the environment variables the code reads, because
# ClawHub's analysis flags a mismatch between the two.
/.github/                       @anatoli-iliev
/pyproject.toml                 @anatoli-iliev
/SKILL.md                       @anatoli-iliev
